  {
   "slug": "how-often-should-my-cat-see-the-vet",
   "title": "How often should my cat see the vet?",
   "date": "2026-10-15T09:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Cats are masters at hiding illness. Here is how often we like to see them, at every age.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Cats are experts at hiding pain, which is why regular check-ups matter more for them than for almost any other pet. By the time a cat looks ill, it has often been unwell for a while.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Age</th><th>How often</th><th>What we check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kitten</td><td>Every 3–4 weeks until 16 weeks</td><td>Vaccines, worming, growth</td></tr><tr><td>Adult</td><td>Once a year</td><td>Teeth, weight, heart</td></tr><tr><td>Senior (11+)</td><td>Every six months</td><td>Blood pressure, kidneys, thyroid</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Signs not to wait on</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Drinking much more than usual</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Weight loss you can feel along the spine</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Straining in the litter tray</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Hiding or sleeping in unusual places</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Our cat-only waiting room and slow, gentle handling mean most cats leave less stressed than they arrived. Bring a towel from home to cover the carrier.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Indoor cats need check-ups just as much as outdoor ones. They may avoid fights and traffic, but they are more prone to weight gain, dental disease and bladder problems.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "cats"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "check-ups",
    "senior-pets"
   ],
   "featured_media": "cat-grey",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "rabbit-diet-basics",
   "title": "Hay, greens and a few pellets: rabbit diet basics",
   "date": "2026-10-06T14:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Most rabbit health problems start with the wrong food. The simple diet our rabbit vet recommends.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Rabbits are designed to graze for hours. Their teeth grow all their lives and their gut needs a constant supply of fibre. Getting the diet right prevents most of the problems we see.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The daily plate</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Hay</strong>: a pile as big as your rabbit, every day.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Greens</strong>: a handful of leafy herbs and vegetables morning and evening.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Pellets</strong>: an egg-cup full, no more.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Water</strong>: fresh, in a bowl rather than a bottle.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:food-bowl}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:food-bowl}}\" alt=\"A bowl of food and a treat\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:food-bowl}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Avoid muesli-style mixes; rabbits pick out the sugary bits and leave the fibre. Fruit and carrots are treats, not meals.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If a rabbit stops eating or producing droppings for more than a few hours, call us straight away.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Dr Ruth</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "categories": [
    "small-pets"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "nutrition"
   ],
   "featured_media": "rabbit",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false,
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Omar",
     "email": "omar@example.com",
     "content": "Switched from muesli to hay and pellets and Clover's teeth are so much better.",
     "date": "2026-10-08T17:45:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "first-week-with-a-new-puppy",
   "title": "Your first week with a new puppy",
   "date": "2026-09-25T11:30:00",
   "excerpt": "Sleep, toilet training, the first vet visit and what to buy. A gentle plan for the first seven days.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Bringing a puppy home is a joy and, let us be honest, a little chaotic. A simple routine helps your puppy feel safe and helps you get some sleep.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Days one to three</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Keep things quiet. Let your puppy explore one or two rooms, offer the garden every hour and after every meal and nap, and praise the smallest success.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Days four to seven</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Book the first vet check and vaccination.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Start short handling sessions: paws, ears, mouth.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Introduce the crate with the door open and a chew inside.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Our puppy plan covers the first vaccines, worming, flea care and two extra nurse checks, spread into small monthly payments.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "dogs"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "puppies",
    "check-ups"
   ],
   "featured_media": "puppy",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "dental-health-month",
   "title": "Why dental health matters more than you think",
   "date": "2026-09-12T09:15:00",
   "excerpt": "Four in five adult pets have dental disease. How to spot it and what we do about it.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Bad breath is not just a dog thing. It is often the first sign of gum disease, which can be painful and can affect the heart and kidneys over time.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:columns --><div class=\"wp-block-columns\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Look for</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Red gums</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Brown tartar on back teeth</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Chewing on one side</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Dropping food</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">At home</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Brush daily with pet toothpaste</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Dental chews, not bones</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Check teeth weekly</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>During September, nurse dental checks are free. We will look at your pet's teeth, show you how to brush, and tell you honestly if a scale and polish is needed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If your pet does need a scale and polish, we take dental X-rays at the same time, because most of each tooth sits below the gum where problems hide.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "dogs",
    "cats"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "dental"
   ],
   "featured_media": "clinic",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "meet-pepper-adoption",
   "title": "Adoption story: how Pepper learned to trust",
   "date": "2026-08-30T16:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Pepper arrived hiding under a radiator. Six weeks later she is the nurses' favourite lap cat.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>When Pepper came to us from the rescue, she spent three days under the radiator in the cat ward, eating only when the lights were off. Today she climbs onto the nearest lap the moment you sit down.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Small steps</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Nurse visits at the same times every day</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A covered bed she could hide in</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Treats from a spoon, then from a hand</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Pepper is now ready for a quiet home of her own. She would love a sunny windowsill, a person who works from home, and no other cats.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:buttons --><div class=\"wp-block-buttons\"><!-- wp:button --><div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"{{page:adoption}}\">Ask about Pepper</a></div><!-- /wp:button --></div><!-- /wp:buttons -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Every animal in our adoption corner has had a full health check, vaccines, a microchip and neutering, and goes home with four weeks of insurance and a bag of their usual food.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "adoption"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "rescue"
   ],
   "featured_media": "adopt-pepper",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "summer-heat-safety",
   "title": "Hot days: keeping dogs safe in the heat",
   "date": "2026-08-12T08:30:00",
   "excerpt": "Heatstroke can kill in minutes. Simple rules for walks, cars and gardens on warm days.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Dogs cool down mostly by panting, which works poorly in hot, humid weather. Short-nosed breeds, older dogs and heavy dogs are most at risk.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Simple rules</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Walk early in the morning or late in the evening.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Test the pavement with the back of your hand for seven seconds.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Never leave a dog in a car, even with the windows open.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Offer shade and water in the garden all day.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Signs of heatstroke include heavy panting, drooling, wobbling and vomiting. Move your dog somewhere cool, wet them with cool (not ice-cold) water and call us on the way.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Cats feel the heat too. Leave a bowl of water in a shady spot and close the blinds on south-facing windows during the hottest part of the day.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "dogs"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "seasonal"
   ],
   "featured_media": "hero-friends",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  }
